{"parentUuid":"83c64924-093d-462a-8633-ebb967f789a9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01QQ1ZiNaim1aWqMzHi2FqJ5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- A `context.WithTimeout` wrapping a subprocess call is *observationally blind* — when `cmd.Run()` returns `DeadlineExceeded`, you only know \"wall clock elapsed\", not whether the process was stuck on DNS, stuck waiting for tokens, or producing output fine.\n- The fix for distinguishing \"didn't reach API\" vs \"slow generation\" is **structural**, not a bigger timeout: you need a signal channel from the subprocess (stdout bytes, stream events) so silence-vs-progress is observable. That's exactly why #964 exists.\n- A wall-clock cap is still useful — even with a stream/idle watchdog you want a hard upper bound. But it should be **last resort**, not the primary signal.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n## Direct answers\n\n**Does #1204 remove the 30s timeout?**\nNo. #1204 bumps the *default* 30s → 5min and makes it overridable per-run (`--summary-timeout-seconds`) and per-project (`entire configure --summarize-timeout-seconds`). It's still a single blanket wall-clock cap. No new distinguishability.\n\n**Does #964 remove it?**\nPartly, and only on TTY. #964 says: *\"no deadline interactive (Ctrl+C is the timeout); idle-based 5min watchdog non-TTY with a hard 30min wall-clock cap on top\"*. The interactive deadline is gone; the non-TTY case gets a smarter **idle watchdog** (silence for 5min) plus a 30min wall cap. The idle watchdog IS the real fix for distinguishing — it only fires when the subprocess is silent, not when it's just slow.\n\n**Does the current 30s timer distinguish \"slow generation\" vs \"can't reach API\"?**\nNo, and you can see this in `cmd/entire/cli/explain.go:1219-1233` — when `DeadlineExceeded` fires the error message lists **three possible causes** (\"model slow / CLI can't reach API / API degraded\") because the code genuinely cannot tell which it was. That's a punt. The user sees the most likely cause (slow generation) framed as if it might be a network problem, which sends them debugging the wrong thing.\n\n**Do we already have a \"proper error when summary takes a long time\"?**\nNot really. The 3-cause message is the closest thing. PR #963's typed `*ClaudeError` work distinguishes auth/rate-limit/config/cli-missing cleanly — but only when the Claude CLI *actually returns* an error envelope. A pure timeout produces no envelope, so it lands in the catch-all.\n\n## So what's actually broken\n\nTwo related but separable bugs:\n\n1. **Budget bug** (issue #1198): the 30s cap is too tight for the workload it now covers — multi-provider summary generation on real transcripts. Frequent false failures. **#1204 fixes this.**\n2. **Diagnostic bug** (the one you're flagging): even when the cap is right, the error can't tell the user *why* it timed out. A user with a flaky network and a user with a 10MB transcript both get the same 3-cause shrug. **#964 fixes this** (idle watchdog + stream events surface \"stuck before API\" vs \"stuck mid-generation\").\n\n#1204 alone leaves bug 2. #964 alone (when it lands) fixes both. But #964 is still draft, was last touched a while ago, and is much bigger surface.\n\n## Recommendation"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":122,"cache_read_input_tokens":93462,"output_tokens":4323,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":122,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":4323,"cache_read_input_tokens":93462,"cache_creation_input_tokens":122,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":122},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagn