protection.\n- **Non-TTY default = idle + wall.** CI has no human to Ctrl+C. The idle watchdog fires when the subprocess has been silent (no `ProgressFn` callbacks) for the idle window — that's the \"stuck on connect / dropped network / OOM\" signal. The 30min wall cap protects against degraded-but-chatty failure modes.\n- **Explicit user override applies on both TTY and CI.** If you typed `--summary-timeout-seconds 60`, you meant 60. The TTY-no-deadline default only kicks in when you've expressed no preference. This is consistent with how config defaults usually work — flag > setting > default; once you've set something the default is dormant.\n- **Idle ≤ wall always.** When user sets `summary_timeout_seconds: 60`, both idle and wall must respect 60. We clamp idle to `min(5min, N)`. No need to expose a separate idle knob — keeps the surface at one number.\n\n**Error message split at timeout** (this is the diagnostic fix you flagged):\n\n- Idle watchdog fired → `\"summary provider produced no output for Xs (likely network/auth/CLI issue, not generation latency)\"`\n- Wall-clock cap fired → `\"summary generation still running after Xs wall-clock cap (model is slow on this transcript)\"`\n- Both apply when streaming captures activity; for non-streaming providers (Codex, Gemini, OpenCode, Factory), the idle watchdog has no signal to bump against, so it effectively collapses to the wall cap — error message acknowledges this.\n\n**Renames** (so the code reads honestly after the change):\n- `defaultCheckpointSummaryTimeout` (5min, currently the only knob) → split into `defaultIdleSummaryDeadline` (5min) and `defaultMaxWallSummaryDeadline` (30min).\n- `checkpointSummaryTimeout` var (test-injection point) is retained for backward compatibility with #1204's tests but now controls only the user-override default fallback.\n\nDoes this section look right? In particular, two judgment calls I want to confirm:"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"u