{"parentUuid":"50d5c8b1-6076-4f1d-ae7d-28faaaee63bf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01NHQcnpNfXg58Awn8bV1aQp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question. The current plan already surfaces stderr verbatim for non-streaming providers — but it doesn't *classify* it. Streaming providers get phase-based attribution (\"never sent its request\" / \"received no response\" / \"didn't finish\"); non-streaming providers just get \"produced no output\" or \"was generating output when killed\" plus raw stderr.\n\nYou can do better for non-streaming. Two complementary mechanisms:\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- PR #963 already built a stderr-and-HTTP-status classifier for **Claude only** (`*ClaudeError` with kinds `auth/rate_limit/config/cli_missing/unknown`). The infrastructure exists; it's just provider-specific. A generic stderr classifier for the other four providers (Codex/Cursor/Copilot/Gemini) would close that gap.\n- \"Slow model\" isn't stderr-detectable — that's the timeout scenario itself. The current 2-bucket non-streaming diagnostic (\"provider produced no output\" vs \"was generating output when killed\") already does that distinction. What's missing is the *third axis*: the network/auth/rate-limit/server-error split that streaming providers get from observed phase progression.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n## Two options\n\n**Option A — stderr pattern classifier (low cost, high value)**\n\nPattern-match captured stderr against known phrases:\n\n| Pattern in stderr | Classified as | User-facing hint |\n|---|---|---|\n| `connection refused`, `could not resolve`, `no route to host`, `network unreachable` | `network` | \"couldn't reach provider API — check network/VPN\" |\n| `401`, `unauthorized`, `authentication`, `not logged in`, `auth token` | `auth` | \"provider rejected credentials — run \\`<provider-cli> login\\`\" |\n| `429`, `rate limit`, `quota exceeded`, `too many requests` | `rate_limit` | \"provider rate-limit — wait and retry\" |\n| `503`, `service unavailable`, `internal server error`, `502`, `bad gateway` | `server_error` | \"provider API degraded — try again shortly\" |\n| `404`, `model not found`, `invalid model`, `unknown model` | `model_not_found` | \"model rejected — check `--model` or settings\" |\n| (no match) | `\"\"` | (no extra hint; raw stderr still shown) |\n\nWhen the classifier matches, the diagnostic gains a `kind:` row and a tailored `try:` row. When it doesn't, behavior is unchanged from the current plan — raw stderr shown verbatim.\n\nApplied to both code paths:\n- The **timeout** diagnostic (when the user's `--summary-timeout-seconds` fires with stderr captured)\n- The **non-timeout** default-error branch in `formatCheckpointSummaryError` (today's catch-all \"Failed to generate summary\" with raw stderr)\n\nCost: ~80 lines (~30 of code + ~50 of test fixtures across the five categories). No protocol changes, no signature changes beyond what's already in chunk 7. Worst case (false-positive classification): user sees both a wrong label and the raw stderr, so the truth is still visible.\n\n**Option B — stream stdout for time-to-first-byte signal (higher cost)**\n\nSwitch `agent.RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLI` from `cmd.Run()` to `cmd.Start()` + `StdoutPipe()` + a goroutine tracking first-byte arrival. Adds a \"did we ever see any output?\" signal that's stronger than `stdoutByteCount > 0` because we can attach a *time*. Lets us say \"no bytes for 25s into a 60s cap\" — close to streaming providers' \"stuck before first token.\"\n\nCost: changes `RunIsolatedTextGeneratorCLI` signature, ripples through four call sites + existing tests, and adds a stream-reader goroutine to a previously-buffered path. ~200 lines net. Value is marginal over Option A because the timeout label already says \"produced no output\" when `stdoutByteCount == 0`."}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":