xt) / 4` per delta.\n\n- **`looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag`** requires BOTH a rejection-phrase AND a streaming-flag name. This is the false-positive guard the plan calls out. Verify the AND-logic is correct (not OR, not implied).\n\n- **Fallback recursion safety:** When `looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag` fires, the code calls `c.GenerateText(ctx, prompt, model)`. Verify this does NOT recurse into `GenerateTextStreaming` (it shouldn't, since `GenerateText` doesn't call streaming — but confirm by tracing).\n\n- **Test fake `sh -c` script:** `buildFakeShellScript` heredocs fixture data into stdout/stderr. The `<<'__EOF__'` form preserves backslashes and `$` (single-quoted heredoc). Verify fixture data with JSON doesn't break the heredoc (no `__EOF__` substrings inside).\n\n- **`itoa` reimplementation:** The test helpers include a custom `itoa` rather than using `strconv.Itoa`. Is this necessary, or is it leftover from a context that didn't have stdlib access? `strconv` is freely available — `itoa` looks like dead-weight.\n\n- **Goconst follow-up:** The goconst fix added `streamEventTypeStreamEvent` constant. Confirm it's used consistently — both in production (`makeProgressDispatcher`) and tests — and that no new repeated string literals snuck in (look for `\"system\"`, `\"requesting\"`, `\"message_start\"`, `\"content_block_delta\"`, `\"haiku\"` — these are candidates).\n\n## File structure check\n\n`generate_streaming.go` is ~190 lines and contains the streaming method + 4 helpers. Is this the right level of decomposition, or are the helpers heavy enough to warrant their own file (e.g., `stream_progress.go` for `makeProgressDispatcher`)?\n\n`generate_streaming_test.go` is ~170 lines and includes test infrastructure (`fakeStreamCmd`, `buildFakeShellScript`, `itoa`). Is the test-infrastructure code worth extracting to a shared `claudecode_testutil.go` (anticipating future test additions in chunk 4/5/6/7/8 that might need similar fakes)?\n\n## Return format\n\n**Strengths:** [what's well-done]\n**Issues:**\n- **Critical:** [blockers]\n- **Important:** [should be addressed]\n- **Minor:** [polish]\n**Assessment:** Approved | Changes Required","agentId":"a11403c20e1596a2c","agentType":"superpowers:code-reviewer","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have all the information needed for a complete review. Let me write the full assessment.\n\n---\n\n**Strengths:**\n\nThe implementation is a clean, plan-faithful translation. Several design choices deserve recognition:\n\n1. **Subprocess lifecycle order is correct.** `cmd.Start()` → `streamClaudeResponse(stdout, ...)` → `io.Copy(io.Discard, stdout)` → `cmd.Wait()` is exactly right. The drain is placed after the scanner exits and before `Wait`, which is the only safe position. The comment explaining why (`ErrTooLong` on oversized lines) is precise.\n\n2. **Env/dir isolation is symmetric with `GenerateText`.** `cmd.Dir = os.TempDir()`, `cmd.Env = agent.StripGitEnv(os.Environ())`, and `cmd.Stdin = strings.NewReader(prompt)` match `generate.go` line-for-line. A reader comparing the two methods can confirm the isolation policy is consistently applied without having to hunt for differences.\n\n3. **Post-Wait error precedence is correct and matches spec.** The ordering at lines 75–117 is: (1) envelope `IsError`, (2) `ctx.Err()`, (3) successful `final` with `PhaseDone` emission, (4) `waitErr` with fallback detection, (5) `parseErr`, (6) fallback sentinel. This is precisely the ordering the plan specifies, and the comment \"Specific envelope error outranks a generic ctx-cancel message\" gives the reader the right mental model immediately.\n\n4. **`makeProgressDispatcher` is closure-local.** `outputTokensEstimate` is declared inside the returned closure factory, not at the `GenerateTextStreaming` level. Each call to `makeProgressDispatcher` gets its own accumulator with no sharing risk.\n\n5. **`looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag` AND-logic is correct.** The function short-circuits on missing rejection phrase (`return false`) before checking for streaming flag names. This is the AND, not OR, and it eliminates false positives on unrelated errors.\n\n6. **Fallback does not recurse.** `c.GenerateText(ctx, prompt, model)` calls the non-streaming path in `generate.go`, which has no reference to `GenerateTextStreaming`. The call chain is a dead end. Confirmed by grep.\n\n7. **Heredoc safety.** The `__EOF__` delimiter does not appear in any of the three fixture files. The single-quoted `<<'__EOF__'` form is correct for preserving `$` and backslash characters in NDJSON. The delimiter collision check passes.\n\n8. **`streamEventTypeStreamEvent` is applied consistently.** The constant is used in both the production dispatcher (lines 146, 153) and in the updated `response_test.go` (lines 150, 152). The `response_test.go` diff is clean — only the two literal-to-constant substitutions, no collateral changes. Lint is clean.\n\n9. **Test name rename is accurate.** `TestGenerateTextStreaming_EnvelopeErrorSurfaced` is a better name than `EnvelopeErrorOverridesCancel` given that the pre-cancelled context approach does not deterministically exercise the precedence. The docstring is honest about what the test does and does not prove, and correctly points to code inspection for the ordering guarantee.\n\n---\n\n**Issues:**\n\n**Critical:** None.\n\n**Important:**\n\n1. **`itoa` reimplementation is dead weight.** `/cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/generate_streaming_test.go` lines 166–186 implement a full integer-to-string converter. `strconv` is freely available and already imported in the package (or trivially importable — it is part of the Go standard library with no dependency cost). The only call site is `buildFakeShellScript` line 160, where `itoa(exitCode)` could be replaced with `strconv.Itoa(exitCode)`. The custom `itoa` is not a goconst/dupl violation (it's test-only), but it is unnecessary complexity that a future maintainer will have to read and understand. It also handles negative numbers, which exit codes in this context will never be. Replace with `strconv.Itoa`.\n\n2. **`\"verbose\"` in `looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag` is technically overbroad.** The function correctly requires AND (rejection phrase + streaming flag name), which is the plan's false-positive guard. However, `\"verbose\"` alone as a flag name is less specific than `\"stream-json\"`. A hypothetical older Claude CLI that rejected `--verbose` for a completely different reason (say, a logging regression unrelated to streaming) would still trigger fallback. The guard holds as long as the command being diagnosed is the streaming command, which is always true here. But `\"output-format\"` would be a more precise discriminator than `\"verbose\"`: if the CLI rejected `--output-format stream-json`, the error will almost certainly contain `\"output-format\"` or `\"stream-json\"`, not just `\"verbose\"`. The current implementation matches the plan verbatim, so this is not a deviation, but it is a latent precision issue. Consider replacing `strings.Contains(lower, \"verbose\")` with `strings.Contains(lower, \"output-format\")` for tighter targeting. This is not urgent, but it avoids an unintended fallback if `--verbose` becomes a user-side issue in a future Claude CLI release.\n\n3. **`include-partial` is checked but never passed as a flag.** `looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag` line 186 checks for `\"include-partial\"` in stderr, but the command construction at lines 38–43 does not include `--include-partial`. This is not harmful — the check is dead code in practice, and it was copied verbatim from the plan. If `--include-partial` was considered for future use and preemptively added to the detector, that intent should be documented with a comment. Otherwise, remove it to keep the detector aligned with what is actually sent.\n\n**Minor:**\n\n1. **No direct unit tests for `looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag`, `envelopeErrorMessage`, or `outputTokensFromUsage`.** These helpers are exercised indirectly through the integration-style tests (the fake-subprocess tests), but they are pure functions that are trivially unit-testable. A table-driven test for `looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag` in particular would document the accepted/rejected patterns and protect against future changes. This is not required for the chunk (the plan does not mandate it), but it is a gap that chunk 4 or 5 could fill.\n\n2. **`equalPhases` in the test file duplicates `equalStrings` from `response_test.go`.** Both are two-file private helpers doing the same slice-equality loop. The only difference is the element type (`agent.ProgressPhase` vs `string`). Since both live in `package claudecode`, they could be unified with generics (`func equalSlices[T comparable](a, b []T) bool`). The current duplication is below the 75-token lint threshold (each is ~8 lines), so it does not block CI. Extracting to a shared `claudecode_testutil.go` or using a generic helper would pay off when chunk 4/5 add more test helpers.\n\n3. **Test file imports `path/filepath` and `os` but `fakeStreamCmd` reads fixtures by name.** `stream_session.jsonl` is present in `testdata/` but unused by any test in this chunk. This is not a code quality issue — the fixture was likely added in anticipation of a session-streaming test — but it is worth noting for the chunk 4 reviewer.\n\n---\n\n**Assessment: Approved**\n\nThe implementation is correct, well-structured, and plan-faithful. The two Important items (`itoa` replacement and `include-partial` dead check) are straightforward cleanup items. The `\"verbose\"` precision issue is a latent risk rather than a current bug. None of these block merging or the production callsite addition in chunk 4. The subprocess lifecycle, error ordering, and fallback safety are all verified correct."}],"totalDurationMs":886568,"totalTokens":62409,"totalToolUseCount":59,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":283,"cache_read_input_tokens":60205,"output_tokens":1920,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0