n1173\t\tif flagSeconds > 0 {\n1174\t\t\treturn time.Duration(flagSeconds) * time.Second\n1175\t\t}\n1176\t\ts, err := settings.Load(ctx)\n1177\t\tif err != nil {\n1178\t\t\tlogging.Debug(ctx, \"summary timeout: settings load failed; no deadline\",\n1179\t\t\t\tslog.String(\"error\", err.Error()))\n1180\t\t\treturn 0\n1181\t\t}\n1182\t\tif v := s.SummaryTimeoutValue(); v > 0 {\n1183\t\t\treturn v\n1184\t\t}\n1185\t\treturn 0\n1186\t}\n1187\t```\n1188\t\n1189\t### Task 5.2: Skip `context.WithTimeout` when `timeout == 0`\n1190\t\n1191\t**Files:**\n1192\t- Modify: `cmd/entire/cli/explain.go`\n1193\t\n1194\t- [ ] **Step 1:** Locate `generateCheckpointAISummary` (search for `func generateCheckpointAISummary`).\n1195\t\n1196\t- [ ] **Step 2:** Find the line:\n1197\t\n1198\t```go\n1199\ttimeoutCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(ctx, checkpointSummaryTimeout)\n1200\t```\n1201\t\n1202\t(Or whatever PR #1204 left it as — likely `resolveSummaryTimeout(...)` is passed in.) Replace the timeout-wrapping block with:\n1203\t\n1204\t```go\n1205\trunCtx := ctx\n1206\tvar cancel context.CancelFunc\n1207\tif timeout > 0 {\n1208\t    runCtx, cancel = context.WithTimeout(ctx, timeout)\n1209\t    defer cancel()\n1210\t}\n1211\t```\n1212\t\n1213\tWhere `timeout time.Duration` is the value computed via `resolveSummaryTimeout(ctx, summaryTimeoutSeconds)` at the call site. If PR #1204's plumbing currently passes the seconds count into `generateCheckpointAISummary`, change it to pass a `time.Duration` (already resolved) so the no-timeout case stays explicit.\n1214\t\n1215\t- [ ] **Step 3:** Update the error-message branches inside `generateCheckpointAISummary` so they only mention a duration when one applied. With `timeout == 0`, `DeadlineExceeded` can still arrive from a parent context bounded externally — in that case the wrapped error must NOT claim \"after 0s\". Guard with the same pattern used in `formatCheckpointSummaryError` (Task 7.2 Step 2):\n1216\t\n1217\t```go\n1218\tif errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded) || (cancel != nil && errors.Is(runCtx.Err(), context.DeadlineExceeded)) {\n1219\t    if timeout > 0 {\n1220\t        return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"summary generation timed out after %s: %w\", formatSummaryTimeout(timeout), context.DeadlineExceeded)\n1221\t    }\n1222\t    return nil, fmt.Errorf(\"summary generation timed out (parent context deadline): %w\", context.DeadlineExceeded)\n1223\t}\n1224\t```\n1225\t\n1226\t- [ ] **Step 4:** Run `mise run fmt`. Lint will report unused-import errors if any timeout-related imports become unused; remove them.\n1227\t\n1228\t### Task 5.3: Update lock-in test and existing timeout tests\n1229\t\n1230\t**Files:**\n1231\t- Modify: `cmd/entire/cli/explain_test.go`\n1232\t\n1233\t- [ ] **Step 1:** Verify the test that needs to be replaced. Run:\n1234\t\n1235\t```bash\n1236\tgrep -n \"TestDefaultCheckpointSummaryTimeout\\|TestResolveSummaryTimeout\" cmd/entire/cli/explain_test.go\n1237\t```\n1238\t\n1239\tExpected (verified at plan-writing time): five tests, with `TestDefaultCheckpointSummaryTimeout_IsFiveMinutes` around line 1147 and four `TestResolveSummaryTimeout_*` tests at lines 1092, 1105, 1118, 1131. The four resolver tests stay; the lock-in test is the one to replace. If the grep returns different names (e.g., PR #1204 was renamed before merge), adjust the replacement target before continuing.\n1240\t\n1241\t- [ ] **Step 2:** Delete `TestDefaultCheckpointSummaryTimeout_IsFiveMinutes`.\n1242\t\n1243\t- [ ] **Step 3:** Replace it with a new test that locks in the new default. Note: this test cannot be `t.Parallel()` because `t.Chdir` mutates process-global state — Go's test framework panics if `os.Chdir` is called after `t.Parallel()`. The rest of the package keeps its parallelism; only this one test runs serially.\n1244\t\n1245\t```go\n1246\t// TestResolveSummaryTimeout_DefaultZero locks in that with no flag and no\n1247\t// settings file, --generate has no automatic deadline. The opt-in surface\n1248\t