res + user set 60s but parent was actually 30s → fires at 30s, but err message says \"after 60s\" — INCORRECT? Or acceptable because user expressed a desire for 60s and the parent ctx narrowed it without our knowing?\n  \n  This last case is a real subtlety. Verify the actual produced error message. If the implementation only reports the user-set value when both fire, is that misleading?\n\n- **`cancel != nil` check on the DeadlineExceeded path:**\n  The condition `errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded) || (cancel != nil && errors.Is(runCtx.Err(), context.DeadlineExceeded))` has an odd second clause. When `cancel == nil` (i.e., we didn't wrap with WithTimeout), `runCtx == ctx`, so `runCtx.Err()` is the parent's err. `errors.Is(parent.Err, context.DeadlineExceeded)` is the same thing the first clause would catch via `err` (which is the error returned from `generateTranscriptSummary`). Is the second clause redundant? Could it be simplified to just `errors.Is(err, context.DeadlineExceeded)`?\n\n- **Sequence of `formatCheckpointSummaryError` call site update:** The plan said the caller in `generateCheckpointSummary` previously did `formatCheckpointSummaryError(err, appliedDeadline)`. After this chunk, `appliedDeadline` doesn't exist — should pass `timeout` instead. Verify the call now passes `timeout`. (Chunk 7 will rebuild this to pass `*summaryAttempt`, but for chunk 5 the simpler change to `timeout` is the bridge.)\n\n- **`t.Parallel()` discipline on new tests:**\n  - `TestResolveSummaryTimeout_DefaultZero` — uses `t.Chdir`, so NO `t.Parallel()`. Verify the docstring mentions this constraint.\n  - `TestGenerateCheckpointAISummary_NoTimeoutInheritsParent` — should call `t.Parallel()` unless it mutates the `generateTranscriptSummary` package var. Verify.\n\n- **Removed lock-in test rationale:** Was `TestDefaultCheckpointSummaryTimeout_IsFiveMinutes` the ONLY test from PR #1204 that locked in the default duration? Or are there other places where `5*time.Minute` is ass