ced zero-default. `generateCheckpointAISummary` covers timeout application, parent-context inheritance, `*ClaudeError` preservation when ctx is done, and cancellation sentinel routing. The `AsStreamingTextGenerator` capability tests mirror the established pattern for all other capability helpers.\n\nThe backwards-compatibility story for `--summary-timeout-seconds` / `summary_timeout_seconds` is correctly preserved: both still gate through `resolveSummaryTimeout`, they still bind via `settings.SummaryTimeoutValue()`, and the `TestResolveSummaryTimeout_FlagOverridesSetting` / `_SettingHonoredWhenFlagUnset` tests pin the behaviour.\n\nThe NDJSON drain after early abort (`io.Copy(io.Discard, stdout)`) is a meaningful correctness detail — without it an oversized-line abort would deadlock `cmd.Wait()` on a full pipe. Logging the drain error at Debug rather than surfacing it is the right tradeoff.\n\n---\n\n**Issues:**\n\n**Critical:** None.\n\n**Important:**\n\n1. **Stale flag help text misleads users about the default.** `/cmd/entire/cli/explain.go:416` reads:\n\n   `\"Hard deadline in seconds for --generate summary generation; overrides summary_timeout_seconds setting. 0 = use setting or 5m default.\"`\n\n   The \"5m default\" clause is the old behaviour from PR #1204. This branch's entire policy change is that 0 now means \"no deadline\". A user reading `entire explain --help` will believe there is a 5-minute backstop and therefore may not bother setting the flag when they actually need a tighter cap. The correct text is something like: `0 = use setting, or no deadline if setting is also unset`.\n\n   This is the only important issue — it's the public API surface of the policy change, and getting the help text wrong undermines the intent of the branch.\n\n**Minor (all documented as carry-forward, confirming the handoff note):**\n\n2. `cmd/entire/cli/agent/text_generator_cli.go:38` doc comment says \"wrap them into a `*summarize.TextGenerationError`\" but the type was moved to `*agent.TextGenerationError` during the import-cycle fix. Not a runtime bug but will mislead future readers of that file.\n\n3. `looksLikeUnrecognizedFlag` in `/cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/generate_streaming.go:186` checks `\"include-partial\"` but that flag is never passed to the subprocess (the args are `--print`, `--output-format stream-json`, `--verbose`, `--model`, `--setting-sources`). Dead code. The `\"verbose\"` check is broader than ideal — any error message that incidentally mentions the word \"verbose\" could trigger a false-positive fallback — but in practice `--verbose` is a real argument passed in the same call so it is the right signal to check.\n\n4. `itoa` in `/cmd/entire/cli/agent/claudecode/generate_streaming_test.go:166` reimplements `strconv.Itoa`. This is test-only and has no correctness impact.\n\n5. `TestFormatCheckpointSummaryError_DeadlineExceeded` at line 173 uses `5*time.Minute` as the deadline argument. The value is now arbitrary (the old 5-minute default is gone), and the test validates `strings.Contains(label, \"5m\")` — meaning it will pass as long as the argument stays 5m, but the test comment no longer explains why 5m specifically was chosen. A future reader might mistake it for a reference to the old default.\n\n6. `TestResolveSummaryTimeout_FlagOverridesSetting`, `_SettingHonoredWhenFlagUnset`, `_DefaultWhenBothUnset`, and `_NegativeSettingTreatedAsUnset` all use `t.Chdir` but none call `t.Parallel()`. The comment in `_DefaultZero` explains the reasoning correctly, but the other four lack even a brief comment explaining the omission. This is a pre-existing gap in the test's documentation, not a new issue introduced by this branch.\n\n7. No direct unit tests for `formatMs` or `humanizeBytes`. Both are small pure functions; both are exercised indirectly through `summaryProgressWriter` tests, but isolated tests would catch boundary cases (e.g., `formatMs(999)` vs `formatMs(1000)`, `humanizeBytes(0)`, `humanizeBytes(1024)`